            <ArticleTitle>The dura causes spinal cord compression after spinal cord injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>582-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3109/02688697.2016.1173191</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>MR scans from 65 patients with traumatic spinal cord injury were analysed; on admission 95% had evidence of cord compression - in 26% due to the dura, and in the remaining 74% due to extradural factors. Compression due to dural factors resolved with a half-life of 5.5 days. These findings suggest that bony decompression alone may not relieve spinal cord compression in the quarter of patients in whom dural factors are significant.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
